# A Scala-style import names a module in the standard library

`import credential.credentialEnv` means what `[credentialEnv](std/credential.ssc)` means: the
LAST segment is a member and the rest is the module, so this loads `std/credential.ssc`
and every definition in it — Tier 0 has no namespaces to hide the rest behind.

The module is a real one rather than a fixture written for this test, because the mapping's whole
job is to land on the standard library as it is actually laid out.

```scalascript
import credential.credentialEnv

val c = credentialEnv("API_TOKEN")
println(c.kind)
println(c.source)
println(c.scheme)
```
